@page "/docs/important"

<PageTitle>Overriding with !important - FluentCss</PageTitle>

<DocHeader Title="Overriding with !important"
           Description="Force a handful of declarations to win the cascade. Important blocks are scoped to a single lambda, compose with states, themes and media, and cannot be nested." />

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">When to use it</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">!important</code> should be the last resort. Prefer the normal
    tools first: reorder utilities, add a state or theme variant, or write a component selector with
    enough specificity. Reach for an important block when you have to beat a style you do not control,
    such as a vendor stylesheet or an inline style.
</p>

<Callout Kind="CalloutKind.Warning" Title="Hard to override later">
    A rule that uses <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">!important</code> can only be overridden by
    another <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">!important</code> with higher specificity. Keep the
    blocks small and close to the reason you needed them.
</Callout>

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">The Important block</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Important</code> takes a lambda that receives a styles-only
    builder. Every declaration added inside the lambda is emitted with
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">!important</code>, and the returned class names carry a leading
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">!</code>. Several declarations can go in one block.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@BasicUsage" />

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    Because the <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">!</code> is part of the class name, the important
    utility is a different class from the plain one. Both can live on the page without deduplicating
    into each other.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@Naming" />

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">Composing with states, themes and media</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    An important block is a normal utility chain, so it works anywhere a state, theme or breakpoint
    callback does. The prefix ends up before the declaration, exactly like any other variant.
</p>

<CodeBlock Code="@Composition" />

<h2 class="@DocsStyles.H2">One level only</h2>

<p class="@DocsStyles.P">
    The lambda does not receive the full builder: it only exposes the style utilities, not
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Important</code> itself. That makes an important block inside
    another important block a compile error, and stops an accidental flag from cascading through a
    longer chain.
</p>

<Callout Kind="CalloutKind.Info" Title="Why not expose Important in the scope?">
    If the scope exposed <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">Important</code>, the first declaration in
    the lambda would return the full builder again and the flag could nest. A dedicated
    <code class="@DocsStyles.InlineCode">IStyleScopeBuilder</code> keeps override blocks flat. You can
    still add as many declarations as you need inside one block.
</Callout>

@code {
    private const string BasicUsage =
        """
        Css.Important(c => c.P(4).Background(Color.Red500));

        // classes: !p-4 !bg-red-500
        // .\!p-4        { padding: 1rem !important }
        // .\!bg-red-500 { background-color: oklch(0.637 0.237 25.331) !important }
        """;

    private const string Naming =
        """
        Css.P(4);                     // class: p-4
        Css.Important(c => c.P(4));   // class: !p-4  (a separate class, keeps both rules)

        Css.Hover(h => h.P(4));                       // hover:p-4
        Css.Hover(h => h.Important(c => c.P(4)));     // hover:!p-4
        """;

    private const string Composition =
        """
        // state
        Css.Hover(h => h.Important(c => c.Background(Color.Blue600)));
        // .hover\:\!bg-blue-600:hover { background-color: ... !important }

        // theme
        Dark.Important(c => c.Color(Color.White));
        // .theme-dark .theme-dark\:\!text-white { color: ... !important }

        // media
        Css.MinMd(md => md.Important(c => c.P(6)));
        // @media (min-width: 48rem) { .min-md\:\!p-6 { padding: 1.5rem !important } }
        """;
}
